WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.980
If you'd asked me 10 years ago

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:03.920
when I started my lab that I'll be,

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:06.120
you know - I'll have a one-kilometre tunnel

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.740
and recording from a whole colony of bats

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:11.080
I'll  tell you you're nuts.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:19.580
I'm studying the
hippocampal formation

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:21.600
a set of brain areas that have to do

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:23.140
with learning and memory.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:25.720
And I'm studying those brain areas in

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:29.120
somewhat unusual animal models - in bats.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:38.060
Bats, I think they are very cute.

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:41.280
The bats that we work with, Egyptian fruit bats

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.760
have these interesting behaviours

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:45.360
that we try to utilize

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.940
and that give advantages over

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:51.140
the standard animal models
which are mice and rats.

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:54.520
So for example if we want
to ask questions about how

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:57.400
3D space - three dimensional space 
is represented in the brain

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:01.860
bats are flying mammals so obviously
they're a good choice for that.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:03.680
Also another advantage is that

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.380
bats have two distal sensory systems -
vision and echolocation.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:13.460
We can trade-off these two senses and ask how
the same spaces are represented under

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:18.540
these two, systems which are
difficult to do in rats or mice.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:22.640
We also ask questions about how very large
spaces are represented in the brain.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:27.440
So we've built this long tunnel - 200 metre
long - and we are building this year an

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:31.980
even longer tunnel - almost 1 kilometre
long. And we have bats flying there

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:34.960
you know 100, 120 times back and forth

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:37.020
while we record neurons in their brain

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:40.540
and we see, you know, very different
representation in this enormous space.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:58.410
So we implant these small electrodes
in the bats that allow us to record

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:02.400
the activity of single neurons
in those brain areas.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:06.480
And we have developed these small
wireless electrophysiology devices

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:09.700
called neural loggers, that are really small
and sit on top of the animal,

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:13.720
which allow us to record wirelessly
in a bat as its flying around.

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:16.480
And then we can have them
fly in a large flight room

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.540
or in various large-scale spaces
that we have built, and study

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:24.620
their representation of space. We can
also have bats interacting and ask more

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:28.600
social questions on the bats.

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:54.940
What I'm really interested in is the
neural basis of natural behaviours.

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:59.860
You know neuroscience has traditionally
focused on either, of course

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:02.160
anaesthetised animals, animals that don't behave at all.

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:07.240
Or if they're you know doing behaviours 
they're doing highly controlled behaviours

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:10.360
you know lever pressing or nose poking in some very,

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:13.200
you know 30 centimetre size box.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.300
The big plus size of side of it is it is highly controlled

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:19.700
so you can get very repeated
very controlled experiments.

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:23.620
The minus side of it is it's very far
removed from natural behaviour

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:26.760
from what the brain evolved to
actually do outdoors. And so I really

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:30.660
believe, in addition to having those
highly controlled experiments, we should

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.960
ideally record the same neurons or at
least from the same brain areas

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:36.980
from an animal behaving in a more natural situation.

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:40.720
Now that the technology allows it
that we have technology for

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:44.200
wireless electrophysiology 
for recording for freely moving animals.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:48.240
You know from wireless optogenetics, 
camera arrays with microphones

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:52.360
with movement sensors all sorts of devices
were not available until a few years ago.

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:57.140
we can really study the neural basis of behaviour

00:03:57.140 --> 00:03:58.900
for the first time for real.

